    classic: true,
    q: "Hotel A runs 90% occupancy and Hotel B runs 70%. Which is the better hotel?",
    a: "You can't tell from occupancy alone. Compare RevPAR, profit and each hotel's indices against its own comp set. If A charges $150 and B $210, B's RevPAR is higher ($147 vs $135) and it services fewer rooms to earn it. A hotel at 90% may also be underpricing: if it sells out often, it should push rate.",
    why: "Occupancy measures volume, not value. A hotel can fill rooms by pricing below its market, which lowers RevPAR and raises costs, since every occupied room needs cleaning, supplies and often a commission. Very high occupancy also signals compression: on many nights demand exceeds rooms, so the hotel turns guests away at prices it could have raised. Context matters too: 70% in a market running 60% is an MPI of 117, while 90% in a market running 95% is an MPI of 95.",
    example: "Say each occupied room costs [[$40]] to service. A earns [[90%]] × ([[$150]] − $40) = $99 per available room night after that cost; B earns [[70%]] × ([[$210]] − $40) = $119. B makes 20% more on 20 fewer points of occupancy.",
    trap: "Don't swing to 'lower occupancy is better' either. Occupied rooms bring F&B, parking and other spend, and a hotel that prices too high can lose group and loyalty business. The answer is RevPAR and profit, judged against the comp set.",
    visual: { kind: "table", headers: ["", "Hotel A", "Hotel B"], rows: [
      ["Occupancy", "[[90%]]", "[[70%]]"],
      ["ADR", "[[$150]]", "[[$210]]"],
      ["RevPAR", "$135", "$147"],
      ["After [[$40]] per occupied room", "$99", "$119"]
    ], caption: "Per available room night" }
