    q: "Which metrics matter most to a hotel's owner, its operator, its brand and its lender?",
    a: "Each watches the line it's paid from. Operator: GOP and margin (often its incentive fee base) and RGI, which performance tests use. Brand: rooms revenue and RevPAR index, since franchise fees are mostly a share of rooms revenue. Owner: NOI after fees and reserves, then cash after debt service. Lender: cash flow against the loan (debt yield and DSCR).",
    why: "Follow the money down the P&L. The brand's royalty sits on rooms revenue, so it cares about top-line share and brand standards; profit is someone else's problem. The operator earns a base fee on total revenue and an incentive fee on profit, so it's judged on GOP, where its decisions show. The owner keeps what's left after fees, fixed charges, the FF&E reserve and debt service. The lender cares whether cash flow covers debt service through a downturn, so it underwrites trailing cash flow after reserves rather than projections.",
    trap: "Their incentives can pull apart: a rate cut that fills rooms can lift the brand's fees and the operator's base fee while shrinking the owner's profit. Managing that tension is a big part of an asset manager's job."
