    q: "A 200-key hotel has $6.0M of fixed costs, a $180 ADR, $40 of other revenue and $70 of variable cost per occupied room. What's its breakeven occupancy?",
    a: "About 54.8%. Each occupied room contributes $150 ($180 + $40 − $70), so the hotel needs 40,000 room nights to cover $6.0M of fixed costs. With 73,000 rooms available (200 × 365), that's 54.8% occupancy.",
    why: "Breakeven occupancy turns operating leverage into one number. Below it, every empty room deepens the loss; above it, each extra room's contribution is profit. It shows the cushion in a downturn: at 70% this hotel can lose about 15 points before it stops covering its costs. It's also sensitive to rate: at a $160 ADR each room contributes $130 and breakeven rises to about 63%. Lenders ask the same question with debt service added to fixed costs, which raises the bar.",
