    a: "Because the calendar shifts: a month can have five weekends one year and four the next, holidays like Easter move between months and citywide conventions rotate. Correct by comparing same-day-of-week periods, using running three- or twelve-month figures and leaning on the comp-set index, since competitors face the same calendar.",
    why: "Hotel demand is built night by night and event by event, so a month isn't a fixed unit. For a leisure hotel an extra Friday and Saturday lifts occupancy with no change in performance; for a business hotel a holiday week landing in the month costs midweek demand. A large convention in the city one year and not the next can swing a month's RevPAR more than any management decision. Rolling periods and same-day-of-week comparisons strip most of this out, and indices handle the rest because the whole comp set sees the same calendar.",
